  "context": "## Tiger Team Compression Model\nThe later decision-before-discovery analysis separates learning a fact from constituting an operating responsibility. Use a provisional, reversible scope for inquiry where appropriate. The move into focused execution should state which uncertainties remain accepted and which decisions the sponsor has actually authorised.\n\nTransformation is treated as a changing compression problem. Different phases require different structures: uncertainty must first be framed, execution then compressed, and institutional adaptation supported without losing coherence.\n\nLarge-scale transformation projects rarely fail because organisations lack intelligence, technology or resources.\n\nAt different stages of a transformation, the problem itself changes. Sometimes the challenge is uncertainty. Sometimes the challenge is execution. Sometimes the challenge is institutional adaptation. Most methodologies attempt to apply a single organisational logic to all three phases simultaneously.\n\nA governance-heavy structure slows intervention. A purely execution-focused structure loses strategic optionality. A highly exploratory architecture layer becomes disconnected from operational reality. Over time, coordination overhead grows faster than operational understanding.\nTags: Authority, permissions and delegation 7.41%; Decisions and responsibility 29.63%; Economics, costs and liquidity 7.41%; Business and management 7.41%; Evidence, provenance and attribution 7.40%; Transformation and operational continuity 40.74%\nCard: https://dakleyer.github.io/perspective-beyond/sources/tiger-team-compression-model-n1292/\nSource document: https://jubap.net/tiger-team-compression-model/\n\n",
